Long press.

208
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:37,520
Yeah.

209
00:14:37,760 --> 00:14:39,920
I think it's long press is Apple's terminology.

210
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:57,840
So what Steve is saying is you go to the app store, you find a review that you like that is promoting your app, mentioning the keywords that people care about, demonstrating the intent that is what's going to get you a conversion in the app, and you long press on that review, and it's kind of a hidden feature, which this is one of those things.

211
00:14:58,080 --> 00:14:58,960
It's a hidden feature.

212
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:05,040
And so it's probably gamed as much as it's actually used by like real people.

213
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:09,520
Because who randomly on the app store is long pressing a review?

214
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:12,720
And then when you long press, it brings up a little.

215
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:14,880
dialogue of, Did you find this helpful?

216
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:16,320
And then you market it as helpful.

217
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:19,040
And that actually influences which ones show up at the top.

218
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:30,320
But how many real people are actually doing this versus how many of your competitors are leveraging this to get the best possible reviews to show up first in the sorting?

219
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:31,680
So that's what you're talking about.

220
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:33,360
And then, did you have anything else to add there?

221
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:36,400
No, I mean, that front on the review side, I'll leave with that.

222
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:43,360
The other edgy thing would be during the paywall view, waiting five seconds to show your X.

223
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:50,160
So that's another edgy thing that people do where you just have the little X just show up a little bit, five seconds, a little bit later.

224
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:52,880
And then that way it does increase revenue.

225
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:56,400
It does work, but it's up to you on whether you want to do it.

226
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:56,800
Yeah.

227
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:03,920
And so then the edgy part of that is that you're kind of forcing people to think that it's a hard paywall, even though it's not.

228
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:11,600
I mean, and so this is one of those where like maybe five years ago I would have been like, oh yeah, that's definitely something I would never do.

229
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:12,720
That's so deceptive.

230
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:14,000
This is the next phase.

231
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:31,520
Maybe we should get on to is that there's a lot of these kind of hacks where, and this is a perfect example where is it immoral to not show the close button so that people who otherwise wouldn't have started a free triree trial, now start a free trial because I think that's the only way forward.

232
00:16:32,160 --> 00:16:34,480
Yeah, for some people, that's probably going to be a little edgy.

233
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:47,520
But on the flip side, one of the things I've been thinking a ton about with freemium these days is that people understand that things cost money and showing them a paywall is a reminder of that.

234
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:55,680
And if someone is getting a ton of value out of your app, is it really that much of a detriment to show the paywall on launch?

235
00:16:55,920 --> 00:16:58,880
Again, five years ago, I'd have been like showing the paywall on every launch.

236
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:00,480
That's like a total dark pattern.

237
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,720
And then I see my fitness pile doing it.

238
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:05,680
And my fitness pile is a great example because I've used that app off and on.

239
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:10,960
I subscribed for a year, tracked my calories really closely, lost some weight, then I stopped using it.

240
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:12,480
Then I subscribed for a couple of months.

241
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:31,760
I mean, just last week I was using it to check in how I'm doing calorie wise and I launched the app, I get a paywall and for me, I pressed the little X button and they don't delay the X, at least in the paywalls I saw, I wouldn't be surprised if they do at some point or do it in other paywalls or something, but I pressed the X and I kind of feel like, hey, I'm cheating the system.

242
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:34,560
I'm getting value out of this app and I'm not having to pay.

243
00:17:34,880 --> 00:17:36,480
The advanced features are super valuable.

244
00:17:36,640 --> 00:17:49,440
And so I have paid off and on to get those more valuable features, but I've been thinking about how the user psychology of seeing paywalls frequently is not even necessarily always a negative thing.

245
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:51,120
So for some people, yeah, it's annoying.

246
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:52,640
Like, ah, why are they showing the paywall?

247
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:54,080
Like, I'm just trying to log my calories.

248
00:17:54,160 --> 00:17:57,920
But then it's, oh yeah, I'm logging my calories and using this app completely for free.

249
00:17:58,560 --> 00:17:59,920
But there's actually a paid version.

250
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:06,560
And personally, now when I'm seeing paywalls, that's my psychology is, hey, ha ha ha, hit that X and I'm getting it for free.

251
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:08,320
So any thoughts on that?

252
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:10,560
Any other thoughts kind of along those lines?

253
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:15,760
I know we talked about personally hard paywalls and I was sharing some data with our app.

254
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,480
Now, David, I was against a hard paywall when we launched this.

255
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:19,920
So I want you to understand the full story.

256
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:24,160
We're talking, my co-founder, he's a developer., we purchased this app.

257
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:25,920
He was like, hey, it's about to go.

258
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:26,640
It has no X.

259
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:28,720
And I didn't know what a hard paywall was at the time.

260
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:29,520
I was like, no X.

261
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:30,480
I was like, why?

262
00:18:30,880 --> 00:18:32,480
That seems hella scamy, dude.

263
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:33,360
Why are we doing that?

264
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:35,280
And he's like, well, that's how we built it.

265
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:38,160
And for me, I like to think of myself as just somebody like that's a growth hacker.

266
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:39,840
So I'm like, look, I'm not going to fix that.

267
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:41,920
Let's let the data kind of figure out what's going to happen.

268
00:18:42,320 --> 00:18:44,960
And so we launched it as it is, even though I was opposed to it.

269
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:48,640
But then I looked at the data and I was like, whoa, that's crazy.

270
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:52,640
We went from one to two thousand to ten thousand just like that with organic downloads.

271
00:18:52,720 --> 00:18:54,160
And I was like, wow, that's crazy.

272
00:18:54,480 --> 00:18:57,520
And so when we had to put the X in there, we dropped five thousand.

273
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,280
Now our app is super basic.

274
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:00,720
Well, you said you had to put the X.

275
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:02,240
Apple made you?

276
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:03,920
Yes, Apple's coming around.

277
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:05,120
And here's how I feel about this.

278
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,040
And I'm happy to say this publicly, obviously.

279
00:19:07,280 --> 00:19:08,880
But like, it depends on your reviewer.

280
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:11,040
Some things are against their Ts and Cs.

281
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:16,000
Some things are just who you get to actually physically review your app.

282
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:26,080
And so while they might say that this is against our rules, like, unless I see it on paper, I see so many apps doing it that I'm like, you can't say this is against your rules.

283
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:28,800
If I see these many apps doing it, right?

284
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:32,000
Part of my job is looking at a ton of apps.

285
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:35,040
And I see so many and I get to distill a lot of this.

286
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:38,960
But we had to put the X and our revenues dropped from 10,000 to 5,000.

287
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:43,760
Now, the reason why I feel like a hard paywall works for us is because it's such a basic app.

288
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:46,080
There's not that many features involved in it.

289
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:56,240
It's a one feature type of app that like once you're in, you're kind of like, that's it or maybe you wouldn't purchase and maybe we haven't done the math on LTV and all the retention since we're forcing it.

290
00:19:56,320 --> 00:19:58,880
But when I looked at the numbers, the numbers speak for itself.

291
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:02,160
So I'm not going to be religious about the fact that this is immoral.

292
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:05,600
I'm going to figure out, hey, the numbers are telling me this is a good app.

293
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:06,800
People are staying on.

294
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:08,560
People are not asking for refunds.

295
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:10,160
So people are getting good use of it.

296
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:12,000
That's the whole reason we bought the app.

297
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:13,840
It's to your point earlier, David.

298
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:21,360
And I think I'm glad you said it too because I feel like, hey, you spent so much time, so many resources, so much money on developing this app.

299
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:25,200
If I can get away with using your app for free, guess what I'm going to do?

300
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:26,800
Use it for free, right?

301
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:29,200
Like, but you spent so much time.

302
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:34,800
If your goal is to just make the world better and have me use your app for free, good on you, mate.

303
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:35,680
Congratulations.

304
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:42,480
But if your goal is to build a business, then you have to start thinking about how can I be more quote unquote aggressive with my marketing?

305
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:45,120
Because if you feel like you built a great app.

306
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:49,200
If you feel like you're actually helping people, then why aren't you charging?

307
00:20:49,520 --> 00:20:53,040
We're doing this stuff for free, but in the end, you're going to have to pay us.

308
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:55,520
If you want to work with us, you're going to have to pay us.

309
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:58,080
If you want to work with RevenueCat, eventually, you're going to have to pay.

310
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:00,560
And so we built a great service that we believe in.

311
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:03,200
And if our service isn't good, it will fail.

312
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:04,000
It will crash.

313
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:05,200
And you can look at the numbers.

314
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:07,120
It's not like we're not giving away a trial.

315
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:08,400
We're doing a hard paywall.

316
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:12,240
And if the trial percentage was very low, then you know we had a bad product.

317
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:15,280
But our trial to pay is on average, it's 35 percent.

318
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:17,520
Let the numbers tell me what to do.

319
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:19,840
It's funny you brought up hard paywall.

320
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:24,560
I wanted to get to that because I'm actually doing a hard paywall in my weather app that's going to get out.

321
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:28,960
Weather Up 3.0 is launching in the next probably before this podcast airs.

322
00:21:29,680 --> 00:21:33,840
But the reason I'm doing a hard paywall, I really struggle with this for all the reasasons you're talking about.

323
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,400
I mean, everything from I don't want to be that app.

324
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:37,760
I don't want to look scammy.

325
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:38,880
I don't whatever.

326
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:47,680
But here's the thing is that with this update I'm releasing, so much of the value is in widgets and complications.

327
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:53,040
So we built a really cool widget for the app and we built a really cool Apple Watch complication.

328
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:53,760
Well, guess what?

329
00:21:54,560 --> 00:21:57,200
That is really expensive for a weather app.

330
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:03,760
And so I couldn't come up with a good way to give that away in any kind of freemium scenario.

331
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:05,360
You can't put an ad in a widget.

332
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:06,400
That's a terrible experience.

333
00:22:06,480 --> 00:22:08,960
I think Apple even has rules against putting ads in widgets.

334
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:16,000
You can't put an ad in a complication., the actual value prop of the app cannot be given away for free.

335
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:21,760
And the app itself is good, but we've been more focused on the widgets and complications lately.

336
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:26,960
And so we don't want to give away the app for free as part of a freemium and show ads.

337
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:28,480
And then the ads is a whole other thing.

338
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:36,080
And like I've been begging and you and I were at a conference last September and on stage I was saying we need for the subscription app industry.

339
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:37,200
So this is my pitch again.

340
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:48,000
The subscription app industry needs a good ad platform that does higher quality ads because I had Google AdSense in my app.

341
00:22:48,720 --> 00:22:50,160
And I kept getting bad reviews.

342
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:53,280
I kept getting support requests of why are you showing gambling in your app?

343
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:59,440
They weren't actually gambling, but it was the slots games where it would be a really annoying animation.

344
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:01,120
And so people are going into my app.

345
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:04,320
I do genuinely care about the experience people have in my app.

346
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:06,240
And the experience was crappy.

347
00:23:06,480 --> 00:23:10,400
No matter how many buttons I pressed in there, people were getting ads for guns.

348
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:12,720
People were getting these casino looking ads.

349
00:23:12,960 --> 00:23:18,080
People were getting all these ads that they found offensive and that were annoying and a detraction from my product.

350
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:24,320
And so even if I could, which with a weather app, because every time you open the app, it costs me money.

351
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:30,560
Even if I could only give away the app itself and not the complications and widgets.

352
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:33,200
I don't have a way to monetize that.

353
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:40,720
I mean, actually the app is losing money right now because we pulled the ads out in anticipation of this revamp and doing a hard paywall.

354
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:48,880
And so I've been losing money for like a year on that app because it has no ads in it and it has a lot of free users and it doesn't have many subscribers.

355
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:56,000
So anyway, I'm going on and on about my own personal strategy, but because I think there's valid reasons to do a hard paywall.

356
00:23:56,480 --> 00:23:59,040
And I wrestled with it, but we're doing a hard paywall.

357
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:09,600
And this is a case where, and I think Apple does allow hard paywalls, but I think you I have a pretty strong argument here of why I need to do a hard paywall in this app.

358
00:24:10,080 --> 00:24:12,240
So are hard paywalls a dark pattern?

359
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:18,000
Well, honestly, I'm doing it because I want to create a better experience in my app.

360
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:40,320
If you start the free trial, you're going to get everything and I'm going to lose money on every free trial for people who don't convert because I'm probably going to lose ten cents to twenty cents, maybe even up to fifty cents or a dollar on every single free trial I offer, depending on how heavy a usage the average free trialist uses.

361
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,680
So actually giving value away for free in that seven for day free trial.

362
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:57,840
So doing a hard paywall, as much as I would have been against it three or four years ago, as much as some people might still think it's a dark pattern, I pretty much have to do it with this app to deliver the experience I want to deliver.

363
00:24:58,320 --> 00:25:09,280
So yeah, that's where I landed on it and honestly from a business standpoint and having talked to you, I'm probably going to make more money by doing a hard paywall than if I did do freemium for this app.

364
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:11,440
And it's something I want to experiment with in the future.

365
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:23,040
If I can figure out a way to do some kind of monetization with a better ad network or something to have some freemium experience, but it's to hard to make that freemium work when you have those kinds of costs.

366
00:25:23,520 --> 00:25:24,560
Yeah, completely agree.

367
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:26,080
Like I said, I was against it too.

368
00:25:26,320 --> 00:25:28,160
I was looking at other like AI tools, right?

369
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:29,760
That help you create content and stuff.

370
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:31,600
It's all a hard paywall.

371
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:33,440
Like, don't get religious about this stuff.

372
00:25:33,520 --> 00:25:36,640
Like, you go to any of your favorite web tools, literally a hard paywall.

373
00:25:36,640 --> 00:25:38,000
I'm looking at Jasper AI, right?

374
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:41,600
Like, I was like, okay, what's a content tool that I love that's an AI tool?

375
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:44,880
Yeah, here, here's what we do, but it's a hard paywall.

376
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:46,720
And AI is a great example, right?

377
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:51,440
I mean, some of the AI apps are offering free trials because it costs them a lot of money.

378
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:57,680
I'm complaining about weather app costs, but like these AI apps often have very high costs.

379
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:00,720
And so even a free trial could lose them money.

380
00:26:00,960 --> 00:26:09,360
So yeah, that's a great example to bring up is that AI specifically, I think we may even see a bigger wave of hard paywalls, shorter free trials and things like that.

381
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:10,480
Is it a dark pattern?

382
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:18,880
Well, when you're paying so much for these AI training and AI inference and running the AI models, it's not.

383
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:20,480
You're giving away value.

384
00:26:21,200 --> 00:26:24,320
If you can't give it away for free, you need to paywall it.

385
00:26:24,800 --> 00:26:25,760
Yeah, agreed.

386
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:29,200
Any other dark patterns to wrap up this segment?

387
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:31,040
Hmm, let me.

388
00:26:31,360 --> 00:26:35,200
Well, here I'll bring one up and see what you think and see if you've experienced with this.

389
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:40,480
I was looking at a few apps recs recently where they're pushing folks to the web.

390
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:42,800
I think I actually got to it through a web ad.

391
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:45,920
So it's not that they're necessarily pushing people to the web, but it's like a web ad.

392
00:26:46,160 --> 00:26:50,720
And so I'm in Safari and the typical like long onboarding thing.

393
00:26:51,200 --> 00:26:55,200
And then I get to the paywall and it's like, start your one week free trial.

394
00:26:55,520 --> 00:27:01,680
It costs us ten dollars to give you a trial, which probably BS.

395
00:27:02,080 --> 00:27:11,360
I'm going to calculate it and maybe I'll do something like this in my app where I'll tell people in the app that, hey, it costs me a dollar to give you this free trial because it is costing something.

396
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:14,000
Now, ten dollars to give people a seven day free trial.

397
00:27:14,160 --> 00:27:17,840
I don't know what app is actually how they're doing the math on that.

398
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:20,080
But then it gives options.

399
00:27:20,320 --> 00:27:24,720
Do you want to pay the full $8.75 that it costs us to get?

400
00:27:24,960 --> 00:27:27,840
And it's like really specific, as if they really ran the numbers.

401
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:32,080
Do you want to pay for the full cost of the trial or do you want to pay $2 or do you want to pay $1?

402
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:35,120
I was like, okay, I'll pay $7, day $1, whatever.

403
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:36,800
Apple Pay comes up.

404
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:37,520
I confirm it.

405
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,200
Now they have my credit card info on Stripe.

406
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:44,560
So then I keep going through all the onboarding.

407
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:47,680
And at this point, I'm just like, yeah, so much text.

408
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:48,640
I'm just scanning.

409
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:49,680
I'm not reading everything.

410
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:52,640
At some point, I hit a button that was like.

411
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:55,680
start your actual subscription now.

412
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:57,440
And actually, I'll take it back.

