00:27:57,840 --> 00:28:05,360
I think it was pretty clear, but I was expecting to tap that button and then the Apple Pay Sheet came up and then I canceled the Apple Pay Sheet.

414
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:21,440
But I tap the button and then they just immediately charged the card like seventy bucks because they got the credit card info with the one dollar free trial, which again, I feel like that was a little bit of a dark pattern telling me it cost eight dollars seventy five cents for my free trial or whatever.

415
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:23,360
But then they have the credit card on file.

416
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:28,640
So then any Continue button, any label that's not necessarily explaining it.

417
00:28:28,960 --> 00:28:33,360
Again, I think the app that I was using actually had the price and it did say something.

418
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:40,080
I don't want to totally throw any app doing this under the bus, but they could technically because this is a web, Apple's not reviewing it.

419
00:28:40,640 --> 00:28:46,800
They could technically just have a continue button that doesn't say anywhere on there and they could charge directly to your credit card after that.

420
00:28:47,280 --> 00:28:50,160
So have you seen this and what do you think of this as a pattern?

421
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:59,680
I mean, not as much, but as much as I like to talk about this black hat stuff, I'm not a fan of all that stuff because sometimes I'll see this on upsell pages you add to cart, you want to add this.

422
00:28:59,760 --> 00:29:02,560
I'm like, oh my God, you're really trying to sell like my stuff.

423
00:29:02,720 --> 00:29:07,360
But fine, I go, no, as long as it makes it clear that I can say no, but I'm trying to look for the no.

424
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:11,280
I mean, I know because I come from the web side of things, right?

425
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:12,880
And so I know this stuff works.

426
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:14,800
I'm not a big fan of those either.

427
00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:17,840
I want to bring value and not try to scam you.

428
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:22,080
And I feel like once you're getting to the scammy, again, I was against a hard paywall.

429
00:29:22,240 --> 00:29:26,960
And so once you get into the scammy phase, I feel like, oh yeah, let's not go that.

430
00:29:27,360 --> 00:29:39,760
But one thing I would say for the hard paywall stuff, if you've got sort of a meditation app, if you've got really good content in the app, consider maybe my friend of mine tested a hard paywall versus a lot of locked content.

431
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:47,200
He has a crossword app and he just made one free puzzle and he tested those two variations because I was first I was like dude, you're giving away too much much.

432
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:49,600
You're giving away too much for free.

433
00:29:49,920 --> 00:29:51,760
Because in the end, humans are against friction.

434
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:53,360
If you make it frictionless for us, cool.

435
00:29:53,520 --> 00:29:54,160
We'll just stay there.

436
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:55,200
We're never going to pay you.

437
00:29:55,360 --> 00:29:58,560
But if you built a good app that people highly engage, by the way, app.

438
00:29:59,040 --> 00:30:02,480
And so you tested that and you saw a boost in revenues by doing a hard paywall.

439
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:05,760
And then you tested hard paywall versus just more locked content.

440
00:30:05,920 --> 00:30:10,080
And so what I've been saying is look, if you don't want to do the hard paywall, just lock up a lot of your content.

441
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:15,040
If you look at the big apps now, like Calm, you can see that a lot of their meditations are all locked up.

442
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:23,520
And so what that gives you is the user can see the variety of content that you have, but obviously they'll have to pay to access any of the content.

443
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:23,680
Yeah.

444
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:29,840
That's a great counter counterbalance because we were talking about the benefits of a hard paywall, but it's not going to work for every app.

445
00:30:30,240 --> 00:30:37,840
Eventually, I'd like to figure out a way to do freemium in my app because there is an aspect to people have gotten more and more comfortable.

446
00:30:38,080 --> 00:30:44,640
I think in apps starting a free trial and realizing, like, hey, I can use this totally for free, get as much value as I can for seven days.

447
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:46,560
And then it's so easy to cancel.

448
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:51,520
And I'm a big fan of Apple and Google Payments because they make it so easy to cancel on the web.

449
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:55,760
It's, I mean, like this specific app, I had to do a chargeback to get my money back.

450
00:30:55,920 --> 00:31:00,000
The one that I was talking about where I accidentally clicked, which is a whole different topic.

451
00:31:00,240 --> 00:31:02,320
If you're doing web stuff, you don't have Apple.

452
00:31:02,480 --> 00:31:06,560
Apple enforcing rules on this kind of potentially more scammy stuff.

453
00:31:06,800 --> 00:31:16,080
But what you do have is people who feel scammed are going to leave bad reviews and are going to do chargebacks with their credit card, which gets expensive and gets you booted from Stripe.

454
00:31:16,320 --> 00:31:20,800
If you go too scammy, you'll find out pretty soon when Stripe boots you from their platform.

455
00:31:21,520 --> 00:31:22,640
Again, it's a similar thing.

456
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:26,560
People complain about AppReview and there's legitimate complaints.

457
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:30,720
I still have lots of complaints that I send to Apple about AppReview.

458
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:33,360
But hey, this exists all over the world, right?

459
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:37,360
You're running a restaurant, you're going to have the health inspector come check things out.

460
00:31:37,840 --> 00:31:41,280
You know, you're running a scammy web app, you're going to have Stripe shutut you down.

461
00:31:41,520 --> 00:31:45,520
People talk about, oh, free market and Apple's very restrictive and I've got my problems with that.

462
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:48,880
But like, don't pretend you can just do whatever you want, wherever you want.

463
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:52,400
Every business has rules that they have to follow and different countries.

464
00:31:52,640 --> 00:31:57,600
They have to abide by the laws of those countries and different platforms that they're working with have to abide by those rules.

465
00:31:57,760 --> 00:31:58,960
And so it is what it is.

466
00:31:59,520 --> 00:32:01,040
So that was a deep rabbit hole.

467
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:05,760
But you were talking about hard paywalls and how that's not always the optimal strategy.

468
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:08,080
So I think you're absolutely right.

469
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:18,480
It's like there are times when having one or two things unlocked and then having everything else locked is a good experience because people actually get to see the value.

470
00:32:19,120 --> 00:32:21,280
It's essentially all these little mini paywalls.

471
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:26,000
You see the little lock button and it's like, oh, I want that, but I know I have to pay for that.

472
00:32:26,480 --> 00:32:30,560
Don't let my hard paywall talk bias you to think that that's the way to do it.

473
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:33,280
Freemium is a great strategy for most apps.

474
00:32:33,520 --> 00:32:39,520
And again, I think what I was talking about, I think the future of subscription apps is better.

475
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:45,760
Freemium is finding ways to monetize those users who are not going to monetize via subscription.

476
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:53,200
Because like in reality, socioeconomically and just mentality wise, there's just tons of people who are never going to pay for your app.

477
00:32:53,840 --> 00:33:03,520
And so the best thing we can do as an industry is be able to give more value to those who just aren't going to pay and find ways to monetize.

478
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:08,560
And then, yeah, lock features, lock premium content, lock what makes sense.

479
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:13,520
And every app needs to explore what actually makes sense to lock versus free.

480
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:17,120
And hard paywall is kind of a cop out, like just lock everything.

481
00:33:17,600 --> 00:33:19,040
But in some ways, a good starting point.

482
00:33:19,200 --> 00:33:24,240
And then now, potentially, I get to work backwards of like, okay, now how can I give away something for free?

483
00:33:24,560 --> 00:33:31,600
But yeah, I think freemium is the right path forward for most apps, but it's just a struggle to make it work well.

484
00:33:32,160 --> 00:34:15,120
Any tips on getting that balance right other than locking content because content's an easier one where like you can give away a couple of meditations you can give away a couple of this or a couple of that but in more productivity apps that gets a little tougher I would say lock up as much as you can I think a lot of times people here we can go off as dangel when people are developing like a to do this app it's like oh I'll ask for a review after ten I'm like how many of your users get to ten really low percent so maybe you should ask for a review right after the first one and then get them or during the onboarding process which we've seen work really well to balance off buying for reviews will show the review prompt during the onboarding process or show it right after a paywall even if they can connect out hit them with the review prompt.

485
00:34:15,520 --> 00:34:20,960
And so what I would say is, I think too many times I look at developers, I'm like, you've just given away too much for free.

486
00:34:21,200 --> 00:34:22,320
You spent all this time.

487
00:34:22,560 --> 00:34:26,480
You're so proud of this app, but yet you're so afraid of charging for it.

488
00:34:26,640 --> 00:34:27,600
Look, I've been there.

489
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:28,640
I feel the same way.

490
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:32,240
I'm trying to increase our prices and I'm like, I don't want to do that yet.

491
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:37,600
I still want to be really affordable to the indies and be the most effective and affordable app marketing agency.

492
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:43,040
So I feel the same struggle as much as I'm saying here, increase your prices or be more aggressive with paywall.

493
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:43,840
I still feel it.

494
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:48,000
When it's our baby, it still feels a little bit different to do it when it's our baby.

495
00:34:48,320 --> 00:34:50,400
So I still feel it, but that's why you need other people.

496
00:34:50,640 --> 00:34:50,960
to talk to.

497
00:34:51,120 --> 00:34:52,400
They'd be like, look, this is a great app.

498
00:34:52,560 --> 00:34:53,280
Like, I'll talk to them.

499
00:34:53,440 --> 00:34:54,560
Like, yo, this is a great app.

500
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:55,600
How many downloads did you get?

501
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:55,840
What?

502
00:34:55,920 --> 00:34:56,720
How much are you making?

503
00:34:56,880 --> 00:35:00,880
I'm like, you know, David, we worked on this Android because people are like, Steve, this wouldn't work on Android.

504
00:35:00,960 --> 00:35:02,640
And I'm like, challenge accepted.

505
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:04,160
And so I was like, all right.

506
00:35:04,240 --> 00:35:05,440
I threw an offer out to the audience.

507
00:35:05,520 --> 00:35:07,200
Like, look, I'm willing to work with anyone.

508
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:11,360
I'll give you a discount on some of our packages, but here's what I want, the case study.

509
00:35:11,520 --> 00:35:15,280
And so one of our clients is like, Steve, this is for an absolute prank type app.

510
00:35:15,520 --> 00:35:16,560
He's like, look at the charts.

511
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:26,000
A lot of the stuff that we're talking about, a lot of things I've been sharing from a monetization perspective, I feel, David, is just more psychology based more than, hey, is this the right moral based stuff?

512
00:35:26,320 --> 00:35:29,520
So when you have a paywall, show it during onboarding.

513
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:30,800
We talked about Anya.

514
00:35:31,040 --> 00:35:33,120
She said that was the biggest tip that I would have done.

515
00:35:33,680 --> 00:35:35,520
Once she did that, her revenues doubled.

516
00:35:35,840 --> 00:35:36,640
And she has a panic.

517
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:38,000
She's rooted, panic attack.

518
00:35:38,080 --> 00:35:41,840
So she's like, I'm not going to do a hard paywall because if you have a panic attack, you want to feel relief.

519
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:43,120
You don't want to actually pay.

520
00:35:43,280 --> 00:35:43,920
So I'm like, cool.

521
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:47,360
So she allows people to X out, but when she added it, it doubled their revenues, right?

522
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:48,080
This is against it.

523
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:48,880
And I was like, what?

524
00:35:49,360 --> 00:35:49,840
There's no way.

525
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:53,360
A friend of mine told me this tip in like, end of 2017, 2018 range.

526
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:54,720
And I was like, there's no way that works.

527
00:35:54,800 --> 00:35:56,320
She's like, no, double my revenues.

528
00:35:56,480 --> 00:35:57,120
I just let the data.

529
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:02,000
And I think too many times we as experts, you and I as experts, we tend to say what is our opinion.

530
00:36:02,320 --> 00:36:05,680
And so I always want to differentiate when I'm saying, like, hey, this is my opinion.

531
00:36:05,920 --> 00:36:10,560
Ask David too, or ask other people that might have the same thing, or this is data driven.

532
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:15,360
And so I try to show data when I'm like, look, I feel confident that this works.

533
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:17,280
And a lot of times people ask me, Are you sure?

534
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:18,560
And I'm like, Look, I wait.

535
00:36:18,720 --> 00:36:19,520
I don't just share everything.

536
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:20,560
I wait, dude.

537
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:24,160
I wait till like two or three, and I'm like, Okay, I feel pretty confident sharing this.

538
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:28,000
Because the last thing I want to do is share something I feel worked once, and it doesn't always work.

539
00:36:28,160 --> 00:36:29,600
Because then people are like, Oh, that didn't work.

540
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:35,440
And whatever you share and however much data you have, there are things that will and won't work for a specific app.

541
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:46,400
So at the end of the day, and this is one thing I've struggled with over the years as an indie developer with limited resources, limited development time, is that I haven't done a lot of testing in my apps.

542
00:36:46,640 --> 00:36:58,000
And it's been to my detriment is that we launched something and it's clearly not working, but it's hard to unwind what you did wrong and then try something new and try something new and try something new.

543
00:36:58,240 --> 00:37:01,200
Somebody was asking me recently, what's the biggest mistake?

544
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:05,200
Actually, it was in the subclub community, a little pitch for the community here.

545
00:37:05,360 --> 00:37:09,520
If you're listening to podcasts and haven't heard of the community yet, we have a private community.

546
00:37:09,680 --> 00:37:10,560
I think Steve's in there.

547
00:37:10,640 --> 00:37:12,000
If you're not, I need to get you in there.

548
00:37:12,080 --> 00:37:12,800
I think you're in there.

549
00:37:12,880 --> 00:37:14,960
But anyway, there's an open question in the community.

550
00:37:15,360 --> 00:37:19,680
It will be a little old by the time this podcast is yours, but it's like, what's the biggest mistake?

551
00:37:19,840 --> 00:37:21,360
And so people are sharing their mistakes.

552
00:37:21,520 --> 00:37:22,400
And so I was typing up mine.

553
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:22,800
Typing mine up.

554
00:37:23,120 --> 00:37:27,200
And I think the biggest mistake I've made is not learning from mistakes.

555
00:37:27,360 --> 00:37:31,440
It's like you launch and you need to then try and find a way.

556
00:37:31,760 --> 00:37:33,680
And it doesn't have to be AB testing.

557
00:37:33,920 --> 00:37:35,840
It doesn't have to be super sophisticated.

558
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:39,760
It can just be, hey, this launch trial starts went way down.

559
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:42,160
Okay, let's try something new.

560
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:54,000
I had a big release with my Launch in a Pro app a few years back to capitalize on the home screen customization made like 20k that month, but then we didn't iterate to maintain that lead.

561
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:58,880
And so to this day, there's tons of home screen customization and widget apps that are making tons of money.

562
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:09,600
And I totally missed the boat on that that because we had this big launch and then we didn't learn from the launch of what was working, what wasn't, and then keep building the features that were going to keep people around.

563
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:13,440
And there were obvious things, but this is what's hard.

564
00:38:13,680 --> 00:38:20,960
If you're an indie out there, you don't have a ton of resources, but that's how you actually grow instead of running the app into the ground, like I have with some of my apps.

565
00:38:21,280 --> 00:38:25,760
Is it when Steve says, hey, do a hard paywall, you do a hard paywall, but then you look at the numbers.

566
00:38:25,920 --> 00:38:27,840
If it's not working for your app, don't do it.

567
00:38:28,160 --> 00:38:32,720
Do something different and don't take any of the advice we share on this podcast or Steve shares.

568
00:38:32,720 --> 00:38:37,520
If he's sharing it, if I'm sharing it, it's probably that we've seen it work, but it's not going to work for every app.

569
00:38:38,400 --> 00:38:38,720
So yeah.

570
00:38:39,360 --> 00:38:40,560
Hey, David, I'll tell you this.

571
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:45,840
The biggest mistake I made, if I were starting the app business today, I would say, I don't care what I think.

572
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:47,120
My opinion doesn't matter.

573
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:55,760
We're going to let the data speak for itself, whether it's ASO, whether it's hard paywall, whether it's trial conversion, whatever, long paywall, whatever it is, let the data play out.

574
00:38:55,920 --> 00:39:01,680
Because a lot of times how we're building new apps these days is we're letting the keywords, because I'm going to be more reliant.

575
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:03,760
I don't want to pay for traffic, right?

576
00:39:04,080 --> 00:39:10,320
So I'm going to be reliant on more keyword organic downloads, but I'm letting the keywords tell me which app to build.

577
00:39:11,040 --> 00:39:15,680
And I want to stay in a field where I know people are willing to convert.

578
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:17,920
There's a painkiller versus vitamins.

579
00:39:18,080 --> 00:39:21,680
So I'm going to build an app that is more of a painkiller versus a vitamin.

580
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:29,840
And from all the categories of apps that we worked with, the mental health space, the fitness, health and fitness space tends to have the highest conversion rates.

581
00:39:30,240 --> 00:39:42,400
So how I'm building my apps moving forward is looking at the categories where I know there's high conversion rates and looking at the keywords and really figuring out, all right, what are some keywords that a lot of people are not paying attention to?

582
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:44,320
So my opinion doesn't matter.

583
00:39:44,560 --> 00:39:45,440
Everything is based on data.

584
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:46,080
Yeah.

585
00:39:46,240 --> 00:39:52,640
Thomas Petty, I don't know if you listened to the last podcast or two episodes ago now, but he was sharing on ad creatives.

586
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:55,840
He's like, look, I've been doing this for twelve, fifteen years now.

587
00:39:56,400 --> 00:40:05,040
He said on the podcast, like, I'm no better than throwing a dart at pictures on the wall in deciding which ad will be the most performing.

588
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:18,240
I mean, when you think about it, that really applies to like your paywall design, your paywall call to action, whether you do a hard paywall or not, you and I have a ton of experience, have talked to so many developers, have worked on so many different apps.

589
00:40:19,120 --> 00:40:23,440
But even then, you don't know what the right strategy is until you try a few things.

590
00:40:24,080 --> 00:40:25,280
And again, bigger apps.

591
00:40:25,440 --> 00:40:27,680
Yeah, you can do super sophisticated AB testing.

592
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:29,600
Smaller apps, just try it.

593
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:30,160
Yeah.

594
00:40:30,880 --> 00:40:32,880
The numbers should be big enough to see the difference.

595
00:40:33,200 --> 00:40:37,200
And this is where people get really hung up on statistical significance and everything like that.

596
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:43,040
But at the end of the day, if you don't see a dramatic change, then pick what you think is a better experience.

597
00:40:43,840 --> 00:40:47,040
But you'll make some changes where it's just obviously better.

598
00:40:47,840 --> 00:40:48,320
Stay with those.

599
00:40:49,200 --> 00:40:50,400
And then just keep trying.

600
00:40:50,640 --> 00:40:54,240
And yeah, if you can and have the resources, do more sophisticated testing.

601
00:40:54,800 --> 00:41:06,160
But even then, I think there's a lot of noise in the kind of AB testing community of when you're AB testing everything and every little thing and how one thing affects the other is really hard to suss out.

602
00:41:06,400 --> 00:41:11,520
And the whole AB test everything is tough to execute in a way that you're actually building a new great product.

603
00:41:11,840 --> 00:41:20,400
You need to have a really good team to not just optimize your way into a crappy app that exploits every possible, like, basic psychology of humanity.

604
00:41:20,640 --> 00:41:23,600
So even if you don't have the resources, you can experiment, just do it.

605
00:41:23,680 --> 00:41:28,160
And don't be like me launching an app, making some good money and then just not following through.

606
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:28,800
Yeah.

607
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:29,200
Yeah.

608
00:41:29,600 --> 00:41:31,280
And just start with the lowest hanging fruit.

609
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:33,440
I've talked to many apps where I'm looking at their numbers.

610
00:41:33,520 --> 00:41:35,360
I'm like, I think your screenshots are ugly.

611
00:41:35,520 --> 00:41:36,640
I think your icon is ugly.

612
00:41:36,960 --> 00:41:42,880
When I look at your numbers, you get great organic downloads, 25% trial activation, 60% trial the paid.

613
00:41:43,200 --> 00:41:43,680
There's nothing here.

614
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:45,520
Why would I tell you just change your screenshots?

615
00:41:45,680 --> 00:41:47,920
Maybe that might impact it, but you're doing pretty well.

616
00:41:48,160 --> 00:41:48,640
